BRUSH DC MOTOR:
Motor type: Brushed DC Motor
Input signal: PWM signal + direction signal
Command units: PWM duty cycle (%) and PWM frequency (Hz)
Driver required: H-bridge motor driver
Feedback available:No pre added encoder but an encoder can be added for speed or position feedback
Holding behaviour: Does not hold position when unpowered. When powered, it can actively hold position only with suitable feedback and closed-loop control.
Typical failure mode: Overheating or excessive current during stall/overload; brush and commutator wear; driver failure due to excessive current
Obvious application: Rover drive wheels,applications requiring continuous rotation and straight speed control
--------------------
SERVO MOTOR:
Motor type: Dc motor+gearbox+encoder+driver
Input signal: PWM
Command units: Pulse width in microseconds uS in degree postion
Driver required: Motor driver and position controller are normally built into the servo; the microcontroller provides the control signal
Feedback available: Yes
Holding behaviour: Actively holds the commanded angular position while powered, provided the required holding torque is within the servo's limits
Typical failure mode: Gear damage, overheating under prolonged stall/high load, excessive current, or failure to reach/hold position when overloaded
Obvious application: Robot arm joints, steering mechanisms, grippers, and other controlled angular-position applications
--------------------
STEPPER MOTOR:
Motor type: Stepper Motor
Input signal: STEP pulses + DIR (direction) signal
Command units:steps and them microsteps by driver
Driver required: driver of stepper motor
Feedback available: open and closed loop available
Holding behaviour: Provides significant holding torque while the windings are energized.Unpowered also have some resistance to rotation
Typical failure mode: Losing steps when load torque or acceleration exceeds available torque. In open-loop operation, the controller usually cannot detect the lost steps
Obvious application: CNC machines, 3D printers, and applications requiring repeatable incremental motion
